## TL;DR  

1. **Upgrade to MapLibre GL JS ≥ 3.0** (the version that understands an array of sprite sheets).  
2. **Turn the `sprite` property into an array** that contains the basemap’s sheet (keep it as the *default* sheet) **and** a second entry that points at your own sheet.  
3. **Reference your icons with the sheet‑id prefix**:  

```json
"icon-image": "my-poi:cafe"
```

4. Make sure the two sheets are **CORS‑enabled**, **extension‑less**, and that the JSON index actually contains the id you are using.  

If you only have a handful of icons, you can skip the sprite entirely and register them at runtime with `map.addImage()`.  

Below is a step‑by‑step guide, plus a quick checklist for the most common gotchas.

---

## 1. Why the icons never appear

| Symptom | Typical cause |
|---------|----------------|
| Basemap icons render, your icons are silent, no console error | The style can’t find the image id you gave it. When an `icon-image` value does not match any entry in **any** loaded sprite sheet, MapLibre simply drops the icon – no warning is emitted. |
| Network panel shows only the basemap’s `sprite.json`/`sprite.png` requests | Your custom sheet is never requested because the style never knows about it. |
| You see the request for your sheet but the PNG/JSON 404 | Wrong URL (extension added, wrong path, missing CORS header). |
| The request succeeds but the icon still doesn’t show | The id you used does not match the name in the sheet’s JSON (or you forgot the sheet‑id prefix). |

The root cause is that **a style can only draw images that belong to a sprite sheet it has loaded**. Until MapLibre GL JS 3.0 a style could only have *one* sheet (the string value of `sprite`). The new array form lets you attach additional sheets without merging them.

---

## 2. The array‑form `sprite` property

### 2.1 What it looks like

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sprite": [
    {
      "id": "default",                     // <-- the basemap’s sheet
      "url": "https://tiles.provider.org/sprites/basemap"
    },
    {
      "id": "my-poi",                      // <-- your own sheet
      "url": "https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi"
    }
  ],
  "layers": [
    /* … your layers … */
  ]
}
```

* **`id`** – an arbitrary string that you will use as a prefix when you refer to images from that sheet.  
* **`url`** – **base URL without any file extension**. MapLibre will automatically request `url + ".json"` and `url + ".png"` (and the `@2x` variants if the device pixel ratio is > 1).  

> **Important:** The *first* entry that has `id: "default"` behaves exactly like the old single‑string `sprite`. All existing `icon-image: "cafe"` values in the style continue to work unchanged, because they resolve against the default sheet.

### 2.2 How to reference an image from a non‑default sheet

```json
{
  "id": "my-poi",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "pois",
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "my-poi:cafe",   // <sheet-id>:<image-id>
    "icon-size": 1.2
  }
}
```

*If you forget the prefix (`"cafe"` instead of `"my-poi:cafe"`), MapLibre will look only in the default sheet and silently drop the icon.*

### 2.3 Minimum version requirement

| MapLibre version | `sprite` array support |
|------------------|------------------------|
| < 3.0            | **Not supported** – only a single string is allowed. |
| **≥ 3.0**        | ✅ Supported. The array form was added in PR [#1805](https://github.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-js/pull/1805). |

If you are on an older version, upgrade (or use `addImage` for a few icons – see § 5).

---

## 3. Preparing and hosting your own sprite sheet

### 3.1 What you need

| File | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `poi.png` | The raster atlas (PNG, WebP, or JPEG). |
| `poi.json` | Index file that maps each image name to its rectangle in the atlas, plus optional metadata (`pixelRatio`, `sdf`, `stretchX`, `stretchY`, `content`, `textFitWidth`, `textFitHeight`). |
| `poi@2x.png` (optional) | High‑DPI version; MapLibre will request it automatically on HiDPI displays. |
| `poi@2x.json` (optional) | Usually identical to `poi.json`; you can omit it – MapLibre falls back to the 1× JSON. |

You can generate these files with any of the following tools:

* **spritezero** – the original Mapbox tool (`npm i -g @mapbox/spritezero`).
* **spreet** – a fast Rust‑based generator (`cargo install spreet`).
* **Martin** – a web UI that builds a sprite from a folder of SVGs (https://maplibre.org/martin/).

All three accept a directory of SVGs (or PNGs) and output the PNG + JSON pair.

### 3.2 Hosting considerations

| Requirement | Why it matters |
|-------------|----------------|
| **CORS header** (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain) | The browser must be allowed to fetch the PNG/JSON from a different origin than the page. |
| **Correct MIME types** (`image/png` for `.png`, `application/json` for `.json`) | Some CDNs will serve JSON with `text/plain`, which can cause the fetch to be blocked by the browser’s “no‑sniff” policy. |
| **No file extensions in the URL** | MapLibre appends `.json` / `.png` itself. If you point to `.../poi.json`, it will request `.../poi.json.json` and fail. |
| **Cache‑friendly** (long `max‑age` headers) | Sprites are static assets; you want browsers to keep them for a long time. |
| **Avoid raw.githubusercontent.com** | That host is rate‑limited and not meant for production assets (see the skill’s references). Use a proper static host (GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, S3, Cloudflare Workers, etc.). |

---

## 4. Putting it all together – a complete example

### 4.1 Style JSON (excerpt)

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "name": "My custom style",
  "sprite": [
    {
      "id": "default",
      "url": "https://tiles.provider.org/sprites/basemap"
    },
    {
      "id": "my-poi",
      "url": "https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi"
    }
  ],
  "sources": {
    "pois": {
      "type": "geojson",
      "data": "https://mydomain.com/data/pois.geojson"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "poi-symbols",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "pois",
      "layout": {
        "icon-image": "my-poi:{icon}",   // `{icon}` is a property in the GeoJSON features
        "icon-allow-overlap": false,
        "icon-size": 0.9
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

### 4.2 Adding the style to the map (JS)

```js
import { Map } from 'maplibre-gl';

const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://mydomain.com/styles/custom-style.json', // the JSON above
  center: [-122.44, 37.76],
  zoom: 12
});

// Wait until the style (including all sprite sheets) is fully loaded
map.on('styledata', () => {
  // At this point both sprite sheets have been fetched.
  // If you need to add more layers dynamically, you can do it now.
});
```

### 4.3 Verifying that the sheet loads

Open the browser dev‑tools → **Network** tab and filter for `poi.json` / `poi.png`. You should see two requests:

```
GET https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi.json
GET https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi.png
```

If you see a **404** or **CORS** error, fix the hosting configuration first.

---

## 5. When you only need a few icons – `addImage` as an alternative

If you have **≤ ~10** custom icons, building a sprite may feel heavyweight. You can register them at runtime:

```js
// Example for a PNG icon
await map.loadImage('https://mydomain.com/icons/cafe.png')
          .then(img => map.addImage('cafe', img));

// Example for an SVG icon (no loadImage, use Image element)
function loadSvg(url) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const img = new Image();
    img.onload = () => resolve(img);
    img.onerror = reject;
    img.src = url;
  });
}
const svgImg = await loadSvg('https://mydomain.com/icons/park.svg');
map.addImage('park', svgImg, { sdf: false }); // rasterized at the SVG's intrinsic size

// Then use it in a layer
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-poi',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'pois',
  layout: {
    'icon-image': ['get', 'icon']   // expects feature property values like "cafe" or "park"
  }
});
```

*Pros*: No extra sprite files, instant iteration.  
*Cons*: Each image is fetched individually, which can be slower for many icons; the image is rasterized at load time, so you lose the SDF‑based color‑theming that a sprite can provide.

---

## 6. Checklist – why your icons still might not show

| ✅ Check | How to verify |
|--------|----------------|
| **MapLibre version ≥ 3.0** | `console.log(map.version)` or check `package.json`. |
| **`sprite` is an array** with a `default` entry and a second entry for your sheet | Open the style JSON in the browser and confirm the structure. |
| **Your sheet URL is extension‑less** (`.../poi` not `.../poi.json`) | Look at the network tab – the request should be `.../poi.json`. |
| **CORS header present** (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain) | In the network tab, click the `poi.json` request → Headers → Response Headers. |
| **The JSON index contains the exact image id** (`"cafe"` in the example) | Open `poi.json` directly in the browser and search for `"cafe"`. |
| **Layer’s `icon-image` uses the prefix** (`my-poi:cafe`) | Verify the layer definition in the style JSON or via `map.getStyle().layers`. |
| **The sprite files are reachable (200 OK)** | Network tab shows 200 for both `.json` and `.png`. |
| **No typo in the `id`** (`my-poi` vs `my-poi `) | IDs are case‑sensitive and must match exactly. |
| **If you use expressions, they evaluate to a string** | `["concat", "my-poi:", ["get", "icon"]]` works; a stray number will break the lookup. |
| **If you rely on `icon-allow-overlap` / `icon-overlap`, they are not hiding the icon** | Temporarily set `"icon-allow-overlap": true` to rule out collision hiding. |

If every line checks out and the icons still don’t appear, open the **MapLibre console** (`map.on('error', e => console.error(e))`) – it will now emit a `styleimagemissing` event for any missing image, which can help you spot a mismatched id.

---

## 7. Quick “copy‑paste” recipe

```json
// 1️⃣ style.json – add the second sheet

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready** example (HTML + inline JavaScript) that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6**.

* 40 + SVG icons are fetched from your own domain (`https://example.com/icons/…`).  
* Each icon is registered at runtime with `map.addImage()` (no sprite build step).  
* A GeoJSON source (`venues.geojson`) contains a `category` property that matches the SVG file name (e.g. `"cafe"` → `cafe.svg`).  
* The symbol layer uses that property for `icon-image`.  
* Collision handling is left at the default (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) so icons that would overlap are automatically hidden when you zoom out.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with SVG category icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <!-- MapLibre GL JS v6 -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.1.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.1.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  Initialise the map
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',   // any base style you like
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco (example)
  zoom: 12
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  List of all category ids (must match the SVG file names)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const categories = [
  'cafe', 'restaurant', 'bar', 'bank', 'hospital',
  'school', 'university', 'park', 'museum', 'library',
  'pharmacy', 'post_office', 'fuel', 'parking', 'hotel',
  'atm', 'cinema', 'theatre', 'gym', 'police',
  'fire_station', 'church', 'mosque', 'temple', 'zoo',
  'airport', 'train_station', 'bus_stop', 'taxi', 'bicycle_rental',
  'car_sharing', 'charging_station', 'toilets', 'bench',
  'drinking_water', 'playground', 'information', 'tourist_attraction',
  'waste_basket'   // ← 40‑ish entries
];

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  Helper: load a single SVG as an HTMLImageElement
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function loadSvg(url) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const img = new Image();
    // Important for cross‑origin SVGs – otherwise the image is tainted
    img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';
    img.onload = () => resolve(img);
    img.onerror = e => reject(new Error(`Failed to load ${url}`));
    img.src = url;
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   4️⃣  Register every SVG with the map (runtime images)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
async function registerIcons() {
  const baseUrl = 'https://example.com/icons/';   // <-- change to your domain
  const promises = categories.map(async cat => {
    const url = `${baseUrl}${cat}.svg`;
    const img = await loadSvg(url);
    // `sdf: false` – we keep the original colours of the SVG.
    // If you want the icon to be recolourable with `icon-color`,
    // set `sdf: true` and provide a monochrome (alpha‑only) SVG.
    map.addImage(cat, img, { sdf: false });
  });

  await Promise.all(promises);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   5️⃣  Load the venue GeoJSON (5 000 points) and add the layer
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
async function addVenueLayer() {
  // Replace this with the real URL or inline GeoJSON.
  const geojsonUrl = 'https://example.com/data/venues.geojson';

  const response = await fetch(geojsonUrl);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Failed to fetch venues GeoJSON');
  const geojson = await response.json();

  map.addSource('venues', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: geojson
  });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'venue-icons',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'venues',
    layout: {
      // The property `category` must contain a value that matches one of the
      // icon ids we added above (e.g. "cafe", "restaurant", …).
      'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],

      // Keep the default collision handling (icon‑allow‑overlap: false)
      // so that at low zooms overlapping icons are hidden.
      // You can tweak the tolerance with `icon-allow-overlap: true` if you
      // ever need every icon to be drawn.
      'icon-size': 0.8,               // optional – scale to your taste
      'icon-rotate': 0,
      'icon-pitch-alignment': 'map',
      'icon-keep-upright': true
    },
    paint: {
      // Optional visual tweaks
      'icon-opacity': 0.9
    }
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   6️⃣  Wire everything together – wait for the style to load first
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
map.on('load', async () => {
  try {
    await registerIcons();   // 1️⃣ Load & add all SVGs
    await addVenueLayer();   // 2️⃣ Add source + symbol layer
    console.log('✅ All icons and venues are now on the map');
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(e);
  }
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does, step‑by‑step  

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | Creates a `maplibregl.Map` with any base style. | Gives you a canvas to draw on. |
| **2️⃣ Category list** | An array of the 40 + category identifiers. | Guarantees we know exactly which SVGs to request and the ids we’ll use in `icon-image`. |
| **3️⃣ `loadSvg()`** | Returns a `Promise` that resolves to an `HTMLImageElement` once the SVG has loaded. | `map.addImage()` works with an `HTMLImageElement`; the promise lets us `await` all loads. |
| **4️⃣ `registerIcons()`** | Loops over the category list, loads each SVG from your domain, and registers it with `map.addImage(id, image)`. | Runtime images mean you **don’t need a sprite sheet**; you can keep the SVG files where they already live. |
| **5️⃣ `addVenueLayer()`** | Fetches the GeoJSON (≈ 5 000 points), adds it as a source, then creates a `symbol` layer whose `icon-image` is taken from the feature’s `category` property. | The layer draws the points using the runtime images we just added. |
| **6️⃣ `map.on('load')`** | Waits for the style to be ready, then runs the two async helpers. | Guarantees the map’s style is present before we try to add images or sources. |

### Collision handling (icons “piling up”)

* The default for a symbol layer is `icon-allow-overlap: false`.  
* With that setting MapLibre automatically **culls icons that would overlap** at the current zoom level, keeping the map readable when you’re zoomed out.  
* If you ever want *all* icons to be drawn regardless of overlap, set `icon-allow-overlap: true` (or `icon-overlap: "always"`).  

```js
layout: {
  'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],
  'icon-allow-overlap': false   // <-- default, can be omitted
}
```

### Tweaking the look

* **Re‑colourable SVGs** – If you want the icons to adopt `icon-color` (e.g. a single‑color theme), provide a **monochrome SVG** (alpha only) and register it with `{sdf: true}`. Then you can style it with `icon-color` and `icon-halo-color`.  
* **Size** – Adjust `'icon-size'` in the layer layout.  
* **Opacity / halo** – Use the `paint` properties (`icon-opacity`, `icon-halo-width`, `icon-halo-color`).  

---

#### TL;DR

1. Load each SVG with an `Image` element (`loadSvg`).  
2. Register it via `map.addImage(categoryId, image)`.  
3. Add a GeoJSON source with a `category` property.  
4. Create a `symbol` layer that uses `['get', 'category']` for `icon-image`.  
5. Keep the default collision handling so icons hide when they would overlap.

That’s all you need to display 5 000 venues with 40+ custom SVG icons in MapLibre GL JS v6, while keeping the map legible at every zoom level. Happy mapping!

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready** example (HTML + inline JavaScript) that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6**.

* 40 + SVG icons are fetched from your own domain (`https://example.com/icons/…`).  
* Each icon is registered at runtime with `map.addImage()` (no sprite build step).  
* A GeoJSON source (`venues.geojson`) contains a `category` property that matches the SVG file name (e.g. `"cafe"` → `cafe.svg`).  
* The symbol layer uses that property for `icon-image`.  
* Collision handling is left at the default (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) so icons that would overlap are automatically hidden when you zoom out.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with SVG category icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <!-- MapLibre GL JS v6 -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.1.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.1.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  Initialise the map
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',   // any base style you like
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco (example)
  zoom: 12
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  List of all category ids (must match the SVG file names)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const categories = [
  'cafe', 'restaurant', 'bar', 'bank', 'hospital',
  'school', 'university', 'park', 'museum', 'library',
  'pharmacy', 'post_office', 'fuel', 'parking', 'hotel',
  'atm', 'cinema', 'theatre', 'gym', 'police',
  'fire_station', 'church', 'mosque', 'temple', 'zoo',
  'airport', 'train_station', 'bus_stop', 'taxi', 'bicycle_rental',
  'car_sharing', 'charging_station', 'toilets', 'bench',
  'drinking_water', 'playground', 'information', 'tourist_attraction',
  'waste_basket'   // ← 40‑ish entries
];

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  Helper: load a single SVG as an HTMLImageElement
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function loadSvg(url) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const img = new Image();
    // Important for cross‑origin SVGs – otherwise the image is tainted
    img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';
    img.onload = () => resolve(img);
    img.onerror = e => reject(new Error(`Failed to load ${url}`));
    img.src = url;
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   4️⃣  Register every SVG with the map (runtime images)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
async function registerIcons() {
  const baseUrl = 'https://example.com/icons/';   // <-- change to your domain
  const promises = categories.map(async cat => {
    const url = `${baseUrl}${cat}.svg`;
    const img = await loadSvg(url);
    // `sdf: false` – we keep the original colours of the SVG.
    // `pixelRatio: 1` is fine for most cases; you can set 2 for @2x assets.
    map.addImage(cat, img, { sdf: false, pixelRatio: 1 });
  });

  await Promise.all(promises);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   5️⃣  Load the venue GeoJSON (5 000 points) and add the layer
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
async function addVenueLayer() {
  // Replace this with the real URL or inline GeoJSON.
  const geojsonUrl = 'https://example.com/data/venues.geojson';

  const response = await fetch(geojsonUrl);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Failed to fetch venues GeoJSON');
  const venues = await response.json();

  map.addSource('venues', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: venues
  });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'venue-icons',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'venues',
    layout: {
      // The property name in the GeoJSON that holds the category string.
      // It must match one of the ids we added with `addImage`.
      'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],

      // Optional – make the icons a little smaller on high‑dpi screens.
      // Adjust to taste (0.5 = 50 % of the original SVG size).
      'icon-size': 0.6,

      // Keep the default collision handling (hide overlapping icons).
      // You can tweak these if you want a different visual density.
      // 'icon-allow-overlap': false,   // default
      // 'icon-ignore-placement': false // default
    },
    paint: {
      // If you want a halo around the icons (helps on busy raster basemaps)
      // 'icon-halo-color': '#fff',
      // 'icon-halo-width': 1
    }
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   6️⃣  Wire everything together – wait for the style to load first
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
map.on('load', async () => {
  try {
    await registerIcons();   // 1️⃣ Load & register the 40 SVGs
    await addVenueLayer();   // 2️⃣ Add the GeoJSON source + symbol layer
    console.log('All icons and venues are now on the map');
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(e);
  }
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does, step‑by‑step  

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | Creates a `maplibregl.Map` with any base style. | Gives you a canvas to draw on. |
| **2️⃣ Category list** | An array of the 40 category identifiers (must match the SVG filenames). | Guarantees we register exactly the icons we’ll need. |
| **3️⃣ `loadSvg()`** | Returns a `Promise` that resolves with an `HTMLImageElement` once the SVG has loaded. | `map.addImage()` works with an `HTMLImageElement`; the promise lets us `await` all loads. |
| **4️⃣ `registerIcons()`** | Loops over the category list, loads each SVG from your domain, and registers it with `map.addImage(id, image)`. | Runtime images replace a sprite sheet – no build step required. |
| **5️⃣ `addVenueLayer()`** | Fetches the GeoJSON (≈ 5 000 points), adds it as a source, then creates a `symbol` layer that uses `icon-image: ['get','category']`. | The layer draws each point with the icon whose id matches the feature’s `category`. |
| **6️⃣ `map.on('load')`** | Waits for the style to be ready, then runs the two async helpers. | Guarantees the map’s style is present before we add images or sources. |

### Collision handling (icons “piling up”)

* **Default behaviour** (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) already hides icons that would overlap at the current zoom level.  
* If you want *more* icons to stay visible, you can set `icon-allow-overlap: true` (or `icon-overlap: "always"`).  
* If you want *fewer* icons (e.g., hide the smallest ones), you can add a `filter` that only shows features above a certain zoom or with a minimum `icon-size`.

```js
// Example: hide icons when zoom < 13
map.setLayoutProperty('venue-icons', 'icon-allow-overlap', ['step', ['zoom'], false, 13, true]);
```

### Tips & Gotchas

* **CORS** – The SVG files must be served with `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` (or your domain). Setting `img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous'` in `loadSvg()` ensures the image isn’t “tainted” and can be used by WebGL.  
* **Pixel‑ratio** – If you also host `@2x` versions (e.g., `cafe@2x.svg`), register them with `pixelRatio: 2`. MapLibre will automatically pick the appropriate one on high‑DPI screens.  
* **Performance** – 40 runtime images is trivial for the browser; the heavy work is the 5 000 point source, which MapLibre handles efficiently with a single symbol layer.  
* **Styling** – You can still use `icon-color`, `icon-halo-color`, etc., but they only affect **SDF** images (`sdf: true`). Since we keep the original SVG colours (`sdf: false`), those properties are ignored. If you need colour‑themed icons, generate an SDF sprite or convert the SVGs to SDF with a tool like **Martin**.

That’s it! Drop the snippet into a page, point the two URLs (`baseUrl` and `geojsonUrl`) at your own assets, and you’ll have a fast, collision‑aware venue map with 40 custom SVG icons. Happy mapping!

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready** example (HTML + inline JavaScript) that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6**.

* 40 + SVG icons are fetched from your own domain (`https://example.com/icons/…`).  
* Each icon is registered at runtime with `map.addImage()` (no sprite build step).  
* A GeoJSON source (`venues.geojson`) contains a `category` property that matches the SVG file name (e.g. `"cafe"` → `cafe.svg`).  
* The symbol layer uses that property for `icon-image`.  
* Collision handling is left at the default (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) so icons that would overlap are automatically hidden when you zoom out.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with SVG category icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <!-- MapLibre GL JS v6 -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.1.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.1.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  Initialise the map
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',   // any base style you like
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco (example)
  zoom: 12
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  List of all category ids (must match the SVG file names)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const categories = [
  'cafe', 'restaurant', 'bar', 'bank', 'hospital',
  'school', 'university', 'park', 'museum', 'library',
  'pharmacy', 'post_office', 'fuel', 'parking', 'hotel',
  'atm', 'cinema', 'theatre', 'gym', 'police',
  'fire_station', 'church', 'mosque', 'temple', 'zoo',
  'airport', 'train_station', 'bus_stop', 'taxi', 'bicycle_rental',
  'car_sharing', 'charging_station', 'toilets', 'bench',
  'drinking_water', 'playground', 'information', 'tourist_attraction',
  'waste_basket'   // ← 40‑ish entries
];

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  Helper: load a single SVG as an HTMLImageElement
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function loadSvg(url) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const img = new Image();
    // Important for cross‑origin SVGs – otherwise the image is tainted
    img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';
    img.onload = () => resolve(img);
    img.onerror = e => reject(new Error(`Failed to load ${url}`));
    img.src = url;
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   4️⃣  Register every SVG with the map (runtime images)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
async function registerIcons() {
  const baseUrl = 'https://example.com/icons/';   // <-- change to your domain
  const promises = categories.map(async cat => {
    const url = `${baseUrl}${cat}.svg`;
    const img = await loadSvg(url);
    // `sdf: false` – we keep the original colours of the SVG.
    // `pixelRatio: 1` is fine for most cases; you can set 2 for @2x assets.
    map.addImage(cat, img, { sdf: false, pixelRatio: 1 });
  });

  await Promise.all(promises);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   5️⃣  Load the venue GeoJSON (5 000 points) and add the layer
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
async function addVenueLayer() {
  // Replace this with the real URL or inline GeoJSON.
  const geojsonUrl = 'https://example.com/data/venues.geojson';

  const response = await fetch(geojsonUrl);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Failed to fetch venues GeoJSON');
  const venues = await response.json();

  map.addSource('venues', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: venues
  });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'venue-icons',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'venues',
    layout: {
      // The property name in the GeoJSON that holds the category string.
      // It must match one of the ids we added with `addImage`.
      'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],

      // Optional – make the icons a little smaller on high‑dpi screens.
      // Adjust to taste (0.5 = 50 % of the original SVG size).
      'icon-size': 0.6,

      // Keep the default collision handling (hide overlapping icons).
      // You can tweak these if you want a different visual density.
      // 'icon-allow-overlap': false,   // default
      // 'icon-ignore-placement': false // default
    },
    paint: {
      // If you want a halo around the icons (helps on busy raster basemaps)
      // 'icon-halo-color': '#fff',
      // 'icon-halo-width': 1
    }
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   6️⃣  Wire everything together – wait for the style to load first
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
map.on('load', async () => {
  try {
    await registerIcons();   // 1️⃣ Load & register the 40 SVGs
    await addVenueLayer();   // 2️⃣ Add the GeoJSON source + symbol layer
    console.log('All icons and venues are now on the map');
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(e);
  }
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does, step‑by‑step  

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | Creates a `maplibregl.Map` with any base style. | Gives you a canvas to draw on. |
| **2️⃣ Category list** | An array of the 40 category identifiers (must match the SVG filenames). | Guarantees we register exactly the icons we’ll need. |
| **3️⃣ `loadSvg()`** | Returns a `Promise` that resolves with an `HTMLImageElement` once the SVG has loaded. | `map.addImage()` works with an `HTMLImageElement`; the promise lets us `await` all loads. |
| **4️⃣ `registerIcons()`** | Loops over the category list, loads each SVG from your domain, and registers it with `map.addImage(id, image)`. | Runtime images replace a sprite sheet – no build step required. |
| **5️⃣ `addVenueLayer()`** | Fetches the GeoJSON (≈ 5 000 points), adds it as a source, then creates a `symbol` layer that uses `icon-image: ['get','category']`. | The layer draws each point with the icon whose id matches the feature’s `category`. |
| **6️⃣ `map.on('load')`** | Waits for the style to be ready, then runs the two async helpers. | Guarantees the map’s style is present before we add images or sources. |

### Collision handling (icons “piling up”)

* **Default behaviour** (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) already hides icons that would overlap at the current zoom level.  
* If you want *more* icons to stay visible, you can set `icon-allow-overlap: true` (or `icon-overlap: "always"`).  
* If you want *fewer* icons (e.g., hide the smallest ones), you can add a `filter` that only shows features above a certain zoom or with a minimum `icon-size`.

```js
// Example: hide icons when zoom < 13
map.setLayoutProperty('venue-icons', 'icon-allow-overlap', ['step', ['zoom'], false, 13, true]);
```

### Tips & Gotchas

* **CORS** – The SVG files must be served with `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` (or your domain). Setting `img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous'` in `loadSvg()` ensures the image isn’t “tainted” and can be used by WebGL.  
* **Pixel‑ratio** – If you also host `@2x` versions (e.g., `cafe@2x.svg`), register them with `pixelRatio: 2`. MapLibre will automatically pick the appropriate one on high‑DPI screens.  
* **Performance** – 40 runtime images is trivial for the browser; the heavy work is the 5 000 point source, which MapLibre handles efficiently with a single symbol layer.  
* **Styling** – You can still use `icon-color`, `icon-halo-color`, etc., but they only affect **SDF** images (`sdf: true`). Since we keep the original SVG colours (`sdf: false`), those properties are ignored. If you need colour‑themed icons, generate an SDF sprite or convert the SVGs to SDF with a tool like **Martin**.

That’s it! Drop the snippet into a page, point the two URLs (`baseUrl` and `geojsonUrl`) at your own assets, and you’ll have a fast, collision‑aware venue map with 40 custom SVG icons. Happy mapping!

### TL;DR  
*`text‑anchor`* and *`symbol‑placement`* are **not** reliable shield indicators – they are either defaulted or used by almost every point‑symbol layer.  
The property that **actually makes a symbol a shield** is **`icon‑text‑fit`** (plus the usual shield‑specific `icon‑image` pattern).  
Switch your heuristic to “*has a non‑`none` `icon‑text‑fit` **and** an `icon‑image` that is built from a shield template*”.  
If you need a fallback for styles that don’t use the template naming convention, also look for the presence of `icon‑text‑fit` together with a `text‑field` that contains a reference (`{ref}`, `{ref_length}`, `{network}` …).

Below is a short “why” and a concrete implementation you can drop into a style‑audit script.

---

## 1. Why the current heuristic is broken

| Heuristic condition | What it really means | Why it mis‑classifies |
|---------------------|----------------------|----------------------|
| `layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined` | `text-anchor` defaults to `"center"` when omitted. Almost every symbol layer (place‑names, POIs, traffic signs, etc.) leaves it undefined. | All those layers will be flagged as “shield‑like”. |
| `layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point'` | `symbol-placement: "point"` is the default for *any* point‑symbol layer (places, POIs, custom markers, etc.). | Again, the majority of point layers satisfy this, so you get a huge false‑positive set. |

Both conditions are **generic defaults**, not shield‑specific. A shield is a *special kind of point symbol* that **fits an icon around a piece of text** – that fitting is expressed by `icon-text-fit`. The style spec even calls it out:

> *“A shield is an icon scaled around its text, and `icon-text-fit` is the property that does that scaling.”* – Skill reference.

Therefore the heuristic should be built around `icon-text-fit`.

---

## 2. The real shield signature

A shield layer typically has **all three** of the following:

| Property | Typical value / pattern | Reason it signals a shield |
|----------|------------------------|----------------------------|
| `layout.icon-text-fit` | `"both"` (or `"width"` / `"height"`). **Never `"none"`**. | This tells the renderer to stretch the badge image to fit the text. |
| `layout.icon-image` | A **template string** that resolves to a badge image, e.g. `"us-interstate_{ref_length}"`, `"road_{ref_length}"`, `"my‑shields:{network}_{ref}"`. | The image is the badge that will be stretched. |
| `layout.text-field` | Usually contains a reference token (`"{ref}"`, `"{ref_length}"`, `"{network}"`, etc.). | The text that sits inside the badge. |

If a layer has `icon-text-fit` set to something other than `"none"` **and** an `icon-image` that contains a placeholder (`{…}`) **or** a known shield‑prefix, you can safely call it a shield.

> **Note:** `icon-text-fit` can be set via an expression (e.g. `["case", ["==", ["get", "type"], "shield"], "both", "none"]`). In that case you need to evaluate the expression for the layer’s default zoom (or for a representative zoom) to see whether the result is `"both"`/`"width"`/`"height"`.

---

## 3. Minimal‑yet‑robust heuristic

Below is a **pure‑JavaScript** function you can embed in a style‑audit script. It works with both plain strings and expressions (using MapLibre’s `Expression` evaluator if you have it; otherwise it falls back to a simple string‑check).

```js
/**
 * Returns true if a symbol layer looks like a route shield.
 *
 * @param {object} layer – a layer object from a MapLibre style JSON
 * @param {object} [options] – optional helpers
 * @param {function} [options.evaluateExpression] – (exp, zoom) => value
 *        If you have MapLibre's expression evaluator you can pass it here.
 *        If omitted, the function only checks literal strings.
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isShieldLayer(layer, options = {}) {
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout || {};

  // 1️⃣  icon-text-fit must be present and not "none"
  const rawFit = layout['icon-text-fit'];
  if (rawFit === undefined) return false; // missing → default "none"

  // Resolve expression if needed
  const fit = typeof rawFit === 'object' && options.evaluateExpression
    ? options.evaluateExpression(rawFit, 12)   // any zoom works for the heuristic
    : rawFit;

  if (fit === 'none' || fit === undefined) return false;

  // 2️⃣  There must be an icon-image that looks like a badge template
  const rawIcon = layout['icon-image'];
  if (typeof rawIcon !== 'string') return false; // expressions are rare for shields

  // Simple pattern check – you can extend this list with your own prefixes
  const shieldTemplates = [
    /\{ref(_length)?\}/i,          // {ref} or {ref_length}
    /\{network\}/i,                // {network}
    /^(?:us|ca|eu|mx|uk|...)-/i,   // common country prefixes (optional)
    /road_/i,                      // generic OSM style
    /shield_/i                     // custom naming convention
  ];

  const hasTemplate = shieldTemplates.some(re => re.test(rawIcon));
  if (!hasTemplate) return false;

  // 3️⃣  (optional but helpful) a text-field that actually shows the ref
  const rawText = layout['text-field'];
  if (typeof rawText === 'string' && !/\{ref(_length)?\}|\{network\}/i.test(rawText)) {
    // No reference in the text – could be a plain icon, not a shield
    return false;
  }

  // All checks passed → treat as a shield
  return true;
}
```

### How the function improves on the old heuristic

| Old check | New check | Effect |
|-----------|-----------|--------|
| `text-anchor` undefined | `icon-text-fit` present & not `"none"` | Only layers that explicitly request fitting are considered. |
| `symbol-placement === "point"` | `icon-image` contains a placeholder (e.g. `{ref}`) | Guarantees the layer is trying to render a badge image, not a generic POI. |
| — | Optional `text-field` contains a reference token | Filters out layers that use `icon-text-fit` for other purposes (e.g. label‑backgrounds). |

You can drop the optional `text-field` test if you want to be more permissive (some styles compute the text via an expression). The core of the heuristic is the **presence of a non‑default `icon-text-fit`**.

---

## 4. Edge Cases & How to Handle Them

| Situation | Why it can trip the heuristic | Recommended tweak |
|-----------|------------------------------|-------------------|
| **Shield badge is a static image (no placeholder)** – e.g. a style that pre‑renders every possible shield into the sprite (rare). | No `{…}` token in `icon-image`. | Add a fallback that checks the sprite JSON for a *stretchable* image (`stretchX`/`stretchY` + `content`) whose `id` matches the `icon-image` value. If the image is stretchable **and** `icon-text-fit` is not `"none"`, treat it as a shield. |
| **`icon-text-fit` is set via an expression** (e.g. only for certain zooms). | The simple string check would miss it. | Pass `evaluateExpression` (MapLibre’s `Expression` class) to the helper so it can resolve the expression at a representative zoom (e.g. 12). |
| **Custom shield naming scheme** (e.g. `"myshields:{type}_{ref}"`). | The regex list may not match. | Extend `shieldTemplates` with a custom regex or simply check that the `icon-image` string contains a `{` and `}` pair – that usually means a template. |
| **A layer uses `icon-text-fit` for a label background** (some cartographic styles do this for “bubble” labels). | It will be flagged as a shield even though it isn’t a road shield. | Combine the `icon-text-fit` test with a **network‑related token** (`{network}`, `{ref}`, `{ref_length}`) in either `icon-image` or `text-field`. If those tokens are missing, you can safely assume it’s not a shield. |

---

## 5. Quick sanity‑check on a real style

```js
// Example from OpenMapTiles OSM‑Bright style
const layer = {
  id: "road_shield",
  type: "symbol",
  layout: {
    "icon-image": "us-interstate_{ref_length}",
    "icon-text-fit": "both",
    "icon-text-fit-padding": [1, 4, 1, 4],
    "text-field": "{ref}"
  }
};

console.log(isShieldLayer(layer)); // → true
```

And a plain place‑name layer:

```js
const placeLayer = {
  id: "place_city",
  type: "symbol",
  layout: {
    "text-field": "{name}",
    // note: no icon-text-fit, no icon-image template
  }
};

console.log(isShieldLayer(placeLayer)); // → false
```

Both results line up with what you expect.

---

## 6. Summary checklist for your audit script

When you encounter a **symbol** layer:

1. **`icon-text-fit`**  
   *Exists?* → Must be `"both"`, `"width"` or `"height"` (anything other than `"none"`).  
2. **`icon-image`**  
   *Is a template?* → Contains `{ref}`, `{ref_length}`, `{network}` or matches a known shield prefix.  
3. *(Optional but recommended)* **`text-field`**  
   *Contains a reference token?* → Same tokens as above.  
4. **If you have the sprite JSON**: verify that the referenced image is **stretchable** (`stretchX`/`stretchY` + `content`).  

If **all** the above are true → **treat the layer as a route shield**.  
If any check fails → **do not flag it as a shield**.

---

### Final note

The style spec deliberately separates **collision handling** (`icon-allow-overlap`, `icon-overlap`) from **shield rendering** (`icon-text-fit`). The only property that *creates* the shield visual is `icon-text-fit`. Therefore any audit that wants to reliably detect shields should **key off that property**, not off generic defaults like `text-anchor` or `symbol-placement`. Updating your script with the logic above will eliminate the false‑positives on plain place‑name layers and correctly capture the real shield layers. Happy auditing!

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** example that shows how to

1. **Create a draggable pin (a `Marker`) at a known longitude/latitude**,  
2. **Attach a `Popup` that opens when the user clicks the pin**, and  
3. **Keep the popup in sync when the pin is dragged** (so the user can see the new coordinates).

Because a `Marker` is a DOM element that lives **outside the style**, it is the right tool for a handful of interactive annotations (the skill you just read calls this the “Marker” path). If you needed *hundreds* of points you would instead use a symbol layer + a sprite or `addImage()`, but for a single draggable pin the `Marker` API is the simplest.

---

## 1️⃣  Minimal HTML

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – draggable pin with popup</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    /* Optional: make the default pin a little larger */
    .my-pin {
      background-image: url('https://docs.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/assets/custom_marker.png');
      background-size: contain;
      width: 30px;
      height: 50px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // The code in the next section goes here
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*You can keep the default blue pin (no custom CSS) – the `<div class="my-pin">` is only there if you want a custom image.*

---

## 2️⃣  JavaScript – create the map, the marker, and the popup

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Initialise the map
// ---------------------------------------------------
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',                     // id of the div above
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you like
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],     // initial view (lon, lat)
  zoom: 12
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Create a Popup that will be reused
// ---------------------------------------------------
const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({
  offset: 25,               // distance from the pin
  closeButton: false,      // we’ll close it ourselves on click
  closeOnClick: false
}).setHTML('<strong>Drag me!</strong>');

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Create the draggable Marker
// ---------------------------------------------------

// Option A – use the default blue pin (no extra DOM element)
const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({
  draggable: true,          // <‑‑ makes it draggable
  // If you want a custom image, pass an HTMLElement instead:
  // element: document.createElement('div').classList.add('my-pin')
})
  .setLngLat([-122.447303, 37.753574]) // start position
  .setPopup(popup)                      // bind the popup to the marker
  .addTo(map);                          // finally add it to the map

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Open the popup on click (the marker already knows its popup)
// ---------------------------------------------------
marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', () => {
  // `togglePopup` will open if closed, close if open.
  // Using `togglePopup` makes the behaviour feel native.
  marker.togglePopup();
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 5️⃣  Keep the popup content up‑to‑date while dragging
// ---------------------------------------------------
function updatePopupContent(lngLat) {
  const html = `
    <strong>Drag me!</strong><br/>
    Lon: ${lngLat.lng.toFixed(5)}<br/>
    Lat: ${lngLat.lat.toFixed(5)}
  `;
  popup.setHTML(html);
}

// When the drag ends, show the popup at the new location
marker.on('dragend', () => {
  const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();
  updatePopupContent(lngLat);
  // Re‑open the popup (it may have been closed while dragging)
  popup.setLngLat(lngLat).addTo(map);
});

// Optional: update while the marker is moving (nice UX)
// (Comment out if you don’t want the popup to follow the drag)
marker.on('drag', () => {
  const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();
  updatePopupContent(lngLat);
  popup.setLngLat(lngLat);
});
```

### What the code does, step‑by‑step

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Map init** | Loads a style and centers the view. | You need a map before you can add anything. |
| **Popup creation** | Instantiates a `Popup` (no DOM element needed). | Popups are reusable objects; we bind one to the marker. |
| **Marker creation** | `new maplibregl.Marker({draggable:true})` → creates a DOM element that sits on top of the canvas. | The skill you read says a `Marker` is “one DOM element, repositioned on every camera move”. |
| **`setLngLat`** | Places the pin at the desired coordinate. | This is the “coordinate you asked for”. |
| **`setPopup(popup)`** | Links the popup to the marker. | Clicking the marker automatically toggles the popup. |
| **`addTo(map)`** | Adds the marker (and its popup) to the map. | Without this the marker never appears. |
| **Click listener** | Calls `marker.togglePopup()` on a click. | Guarantees the popup opens even if the user clicks a second time after dragging. |
| **`dragend` listener** | Updates the popup’s HTML with the new lon/lat and re‑opens it. | Gives the user immediate feedback about where the pin landed. |
| **`drag` listener (optional)** | Updates the popup while the pin is moving. | Makes the UI feel “live”. |

---

## 3️⃣  Custom pin image (optional)

If you prefer a custom PNG/SVG instead of the default blue pin, replace the marker construction with:

```js
// Create an element that will become the pin
const el = document.createElement('div');
el.className = 'my-pin';               // CSS class defined in <style> above
// (or set `el.style.backgroundImage = 'url(my‑pin.svg)';` directly)

// Then pass it to the Marker constructor
const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({
  element: el,
  draggable: true
})
  .setLngLat([-122.447303, 37.753574])
  .setPopup(popup)
  .addTo(map);
```

*Because a `Marker` is a DOM element, you can style it with any CSS you like – even animate it with CSS keyframes.*

---

## 4️⃣  Quick checklist for production

| ✅ | Item |
|----|------|
| **CORS** | The style, sprite (if you use one), and any custom pin image must be served with CORS headers that allow your page to fetch them. |
| **HTTPS** | All assets (style, tiles, images) should be HTTPS in production to avoid mixed‑content warnings. |
| **License** | If you copy a sprite or use a third‑party pin image, verify the license and add attribution if required. |
| **Performance** | A single `Marker` is cheap. If you later need dozens/hundreds of draggable points, switch to a symbol layer + `addImage()` or a custom sprite. |
| **Accessibility** | Consider adding `aria-label` or `title` attributes to the marker element for screen‑reader users. |
| **Mobile** | The default drag‑threshold works well on touch devices, but you can tweak `dragPan` or `dragRotate` options on the map if you need a different feel. |

---

## 5️⃣  TL;DR – Minimal code snippet

If you just want the absolute shortest version (default pin, no custom styling):

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
  zoom: 12
});

const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({ offset: 25 })
  .setHTML('Click me!');

const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({ draggable: true })
  .setLngLat([-122.447303, 37.753574])
  .setPopup(popup)
  .addTo(map);

marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', () => marker.togglePopup());

marker.on('dragend', () => {
  const ll = marker.getLngLat();
  popup.setHTML(`Lon: ${ll.lng.toFixed(5)}<br>Lat: ${ll.lat.toFixed(5)}`)
       .setLngLat(ll)
       .addTo(map);
});
```

That’s all you need to have a **draggable pin** that **shows a popup on click** (and updates its content after dragging). Happy mapping! 🚀
